dts: orangepi-rk3399: describe this board

Everything in rk3399-orangepi.dts that describes the OrangePi RK3399 rather
than fixing a bug someone else also has. It is one patch on purpose: this is
board configuration, none of it is upstreamable, and carrying it as four
separate diffs on one file bought nothing but a fixed apply order and four
sets of context that shifted whenever any of them changed.

The rail-voltage fix is deliberately NOT here - see the next patch. That one
corrects a defect the upstream DTS has, so it stays separable.

Five groups. The full reasoning for each is in docs/patches.md.

1. Boot, and getting a console back
   The BSP U-Boot programs the vdd_log PWM rail from the *kernel's* device
   tree, so rockchip,pwm_id / rockchip,pwm_voltage have to be here or the
   board browns out before the kernel runs - silently, because it is a power
   event and not a crash. stdout-path is removed (U-Boot hangs in
   initr_serial rebuilding its console around it) while bootargs stays, since
   the two serve different consumers. Console is ttyS2 at 115200: 1500000
   reads fine, but the header has no flow control and the UART overruns on a
   burst into the board. Measurements are in patches/uboot/0001.

2. Storage and clocks
   mmc0/mmc1 aliases edited in place rather than added as a second aliases
   node - dtc merges two of them and the merge left the eMMC answering to two
   sequence numbers. CPU rates pinned, and the whole of rk3399.dtsi's &cru
   assigned-clocks list restated alongside them, because a property assigned
   in an override node replaces the .dtsi's rather than merging with it.
   Restating it is not redundant: omitting it deleted seventeen clock
   assignments and left aclk_isp0 and aclk_vio at 12 MHz.

3. HDMI IN
   ext_cam_clk, the hdmiin_gpios pinctrl group, tc358749x@1f, and the fwnode
   graph through mipi_dphy_rx0 to rkisp1_0. The interrupt is
   IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH because that is what tc35874x_probe() requests, and a
   mismatch only shows itself on a rebind. link-frequencies is 372600000,
   which is what the PLL is actually programmed to.

4. VPU
   mpp-srv, vepu@ff650000 and iommu@ff650800 all inherit status = "disabled"
   from rk3399.dtsi. Rockchip enables them in rk3399-linux.dtsi, which this
   board's DTS does not include, so the board had no /dev/mpp_service and
   every piece of MPP userspace failed at open(). Both codecs the KVM runs
   on - MJPEG and H.264 - are behind this.

5. Wi-Fi and HDMI IN audio
   brcm,board-type = "AP6356S" so brcmfmac asks linux-firmware for an NVRAM
   blob that exists. And i2s0 plus the ALC5651 as a simple-audio-card named
   "tc358743", which is the device kvmd's stock janus config already opens.
   The audio path is known not to carry sound - the receiver's I2S goes into
   the codec's second port and that port has no clock - but the card, the
   codec binding and the i2s0 pin group are all correct and measured, so the
   description stays rather than being deleted and rediscovered later.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-orangepi.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-orangepi.dts
@@ -15,15 +15,75 @@
 	model = "Orange Pi RK3399 Board";
 	compatible = "xunlong,rk3399-orangepi", "rockchip,rk3399";
 
+	/*
+	 * U-Boot assigns MMC sequence numbers from these aliases. Without
+	 * them it falls back to guessing, and once it has switched to this
+	 * device tree the guess collides with the device it already bound
+	 * from its own:
+	 *
+	 *   Device 'mmc@fe320000': seq 1 is in use by 'dwmmc@fe320000'
+	 *   MMC: no card present / Could not get mmc 1
+	 *
+	 * The collision is a naming difference, nothing more - 4.4 calls
+	 * these dwmmc@/sdhci@, mainline calls both mmc@ - but it costs
+	 * U-Boot the SD card it was about to load the kernel from. The
+	 * numbering here matches what U-Boot already reports: SD is 1,
+	 * eMMC is 0.
+	 *
+	 * Edited in place rather than added as a second aliases node. dtc
+	 * merges two aliases nodes in the same tree, and the merge kept
+	 * mainline's mmc2 = &sdhci next to the new mmc0 = &sdhci, so the
+	 * eMMC answered to two sequence numbers while sdio0 was left with
+	 * none. U-Boot takes the first match and booted anyway - an
+	 * accident, and one that would have stopped being harmless the
+	 * moment anything iterated the aliases instead of looking one up.
+	 *
+	 * sdio0 (WiFi) needs no alias of its own: it carries no block
+	 * device, so nothing has to be able to name it.
+	 */
 	aliases {
 		ethernet0 = &gmac;
-		mmc0 = &sdio0;
+		mmc0 = &sdhci;
 		mmc1 = &sdmmc;
-		mmc2 = &sdhci;
 	};
 
+	/*
+	 * No stdout-path on purpose.
+	 *
+	 * The BSP U-Boot's initr_serial() honours this property and rebuilds
+	 * the console around the node it names. Doing that from a 6.12 device
+	 * tree is where U-Boot stops talking - the initcall trace ends inside
+	 * initr_serial and nothing follows. The 4.4 vendor tree has no
+	 * stdout-path at all, so U-Boot keeps the console it set up from
+	 * CONFIG_DEBUG_UART and boots fine.
+	 *
+	 * Linux is unaffected: it takes its console from the bootargs U-Boot
+	 * builds (console=ttyFIQ0 console=tty1), not from here.
+	 */
 	chosen {
-		stdout-path = "serial2:1500000n8";
+		/*
+		 * Taken from the 4.4 tree's rk3399-linux.dtsi. U-Boot appends
+		 * its own half (storagemedia=, root=PARTUUID=, rw, rootwait);
+		 * this half is where the console comes from, and without it
+		 * the kernel gets no console= at all and boots in silence.
+		 *
+		 * 115200, not the 1500000 that is conventional on RK3399.
+		 * The debug header is three pins - TX, RX, ground - so there
+		 * is no CTS, and at 1500000 this UART runs at divisor 1
+		 * (base_baud reads 1500000) into a 64-byte FIFO. Reading is
+		 * unaffected; a burst into the board is not. Measured: 1878
+		 * of 1900 bytes arrive, two overruns and no framing errors,
+		 * and all 1900 arrive if the same bytes are sent in 32-byte
+		 * chunks 2 ms apart. A pasted command is a burst. U-Boot was
+		 * moved down to match (patches/uboot/0001), so the whole boot
+		 * is one rate.
+		 *
+		 * Deliberately no stdout-path: the BSP U-Boot honours that
+		 * property and hangs inside initr_serial rebuilding its own
+		 * console around it. bootargs is for Linux, stdout-path is
+		 * what U-Boot trips over - only the latter has to go.
+		 */
+		bootargs = "earlycon=uart8250,mmio32,0xff1a0000 swiotlb=1 console=tty1 console=ttyS2,115200n8 rw rootfstype=ext4 rootwait coherent_pool=1m deferred_probe_timeout=10 clk_ignore_unused pd_ignore_unused";
 	};
 
 	clkin_gmac: external-gmac-clock {
@@ -123,6 +183,14 @@
 		vin-supply = <&vcc3v3_sys>;
 	};
 
+	/* HDMI IN: TC358749XBG reference clock. */
+	ext_cam_clk: external-camera-clock {
+		compatible = "fixed-clock";
+		clock-frequency = <27000000>;
+		clock-output-names = "CLK_CAMERA_27MHZ";
+		#clock-cells = <0>;
+	};
+
 	vcc3v3_sys: vcc3v3-sys {
 		compatible = "regulator-fixed";
 		regulator-name = "vcc3v3_sys";
@@ -173,7 +241,113 @@
 		regulator-boot-on;
 		regulator-min-microvolt = <800000>;
 		regulator-max-microvolt = <1400000>;
-	};
+
+		/*
+		 * Consumed by the Rockchip BSP U-Boot, not by Linux. That
+		 * U-Boot programs this PWM rail from the kernel's own device
+		 * tree; without these two properties it cannot work out the
+		 * duty cycle, settles on the wrong voltage, and the board
+		 * browns out and resets before it ever reaches the kernel.
+		 * The 4.4 vendor tree carries them; this mainline-shaped one
+		 * does not.
+		 */
+		rockchip,pwm_id = <2>;
+		rockchip,pwm_voltage = <1000000>;
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * Pin the CPU cluster rates - and restate everything else rk3399.dtsi
+ * already assigns here, because this override replaces the property.
+ *
+ * A property assigned in an override node does not merge with the one in
+ * the .dtsi; it replaces it outright. rk3399.dtsi gives &cru a 17-entry
+ * assigned-clocks list that brings up the SoC's PLLs and bus clocks, and
+ * an earlier version of this node listed only ARMCLKB/ARMCLKL - which
+ * silently deleted all seventeen. Nothing complained. The clock tree was
+ * simply left wherever U-Boot had parked it:
+ *
+ *   cpll         24 MHz    (slow/bypass mode - never taken out of it)
+ *   aclk_isp0    12 MHz    cpll/2   - wanted 400 MHz
+ *   aclk_vio     12 MHz    cpll/2   - wanted 400 MHz
+ *   aclk_gic_pre  6 MHz    cpll/4   - wanted 200 MHz
+ *   pclk_ddr      6 MHz    cpll/4   - wanted 200 MHz
+ *   gpll        800 MHz             - wanted 594 MHz
+ *   npll        600 MHz             - wanted 1000 MHz
+ *
+ * Everything still ran, which is what made it hard to see. The ISP was
+ * the first consumer with a hard real-time budget: at 1080p60 it has to
+ * write ~250 MB/s, and with its AXI port and the VIO NOC both at 12 MHz
+ * it got roughly a fifth of that through. Frames came back as five
+ * stacked copies of a squashed picture with a per-frame
+ * CIF_ISP_PIC_SIZE_ERROR / CIF_ISP_DATA_LOSS storm and MI_STATUS
+ * reporting MP_Y_FIFO_FULL. Restoring the list makes the ISP write clean
+ * 1920x1080 frames with zero errors.
+ *
+ * The VOP AXI clocks are ours too, and they are the same bug a second time.
+ * rk3399.dtsi's list does not set them (the 4.4 vendor tree's does), so
+ * aclk_vop1_pre stayed where U-Boot left it: muxed to VPLL and divided by 3.
+ * VPLL tracks the HDMI pixel clock, so the VOP's *AXI* clock was chained to
+ * the display mode and came out at 49.5 MHz against the 400 MHz the vendor
+ * tree asks for. The VOP could not fetch pixels fast enough and underran
+ * continuously:
+ *
+ *   rockchip-vop ff8f0000.vop: [drm:vop_isr] *ERROR* POST_BUF_EMPTY irq err
+ *   vop_isr: 156838 callbacks suppressed
+ *
+ * - 32544 interrupts per second on one CPU. Setting aclk_vop1_pre to 400 MHz
+ * takes that to 0 and the errors stop entirely. ff8f0000 is vopl, which is
+ * ACLK_VOP1, and it is the one driving HDMI here; vop0 gets the same treatment
+ * for consistency.
+ *
+ * These target ACLK_VOP*_PRE, the mux+divider composites, not the ACLK_VOP*
+ * gates the 4.4 tree names: the gates are registered with flags 0, so no
+ * CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, and setting a rate on them would not reach the divider.
+ * hclk_vop*_pre needs no entry - it is a fixed /2 off aclk_vop*_pre and
+ * follows to 200 MHz on its own.
+ *
+ * So: the sixteen entries below through ACLK_VDU are rk3399.dtsi's own
+ * list, unchanged. The last four are ours.
+ *
+ * On those two - rockchip-cpufreq is a module now, loaded from systemd
+ * (see docs/patches.md), so between kernel init and that load nothing
+ * drives the CPU clocks and the A72 cluster comes up at 12 MHz. These
+ * rates are what the clusters run at during that window. 1200 MHz was
+ * also the fastest big-cluster OPP the boot voltage allowed back when
+ * there was no cpufreq at all: opp-table-1 asks 950 mV for 1200 and
+ * 1025 mV for 1416, and vdd_cpu_b booted at 1000 mV. Once the module
+ * loads, OPP drives that regulator and both clusters scale properly.
+ * The little cluster is left where it already is.
+ */
+&cru {
+	assigned-clocks =
+		<&cru PLL_GPLL>, <&cru PLL_CPLL>,
+		<&cru PLL_NPLL>,
+		<&cru ACLK_PERIHP>, <&cru HCLK_PERIHP>,
+		<&cru PCLK_PERIHP>,
+		<&cru ACLK_PERILP0>, <&cru HCLK_PERILP0>,
+		<&cru PCLK_PERILP0>, <&cru ACLK_CCI>,
+		<&cru HCLK_PERILP1>, <&cru PCLK_PERILP1>,
+		<&cru ACLK_VIO>, <&cru ACLK_HDCP>,
+		<&cru ACLK_GIC_PRE>,
+		<&cru PCLK_DDR>,
+		<&cru ACLK_VDU>,
+		<&cru ACLK_VOP0_PRE>, <&cru ACLK_VOP1_PRE>,
+		<&cru ARMCLKB>, <&cru ARMCLKL>;
+	assigned-clock-rates =
+		 <594000000>,  <800000000>,
+		<1000000000>,
+		 <150000000>,   <75000000>,
+		  <37500000>,
+		 <100000000>,  <100000000>,
+		  <50000000>, <600000000>,
+		 <100000000>,   <50000000>,
+		 <400000000>, <400000000>,
+		 <200000000>,
+		 <200000000>,
+		 <400000000>,
+		 <400000000>,  <400000000>,
+		<1200000000>,  <816000000>;
 };
 
 &cpu_l0 {
@@ -238,8 +412,32 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * DDC goes through i2c3, so the HDMI controller must not also claim the DDC
+ * pins for its own internal I2C.
+ *
+ * rk3399.dtsi gives this node pinctrl-0 = <&hdmi_i2c_xfer>, which is
+ * gpio4 RK_PC0/RK_PC1 at mux 3. i2c3_xfer is the same two pins at mux 1, so
+ * with ddc-i2c-bus = <&i2c3> the two fight and i2c3 loses:
+ *
+ *   rockchip-pinctrl: pin gpio4-17 already requested by ff940000.hdmi;
+ *                     cannot claim for ff130000.i2c
+ *
+ * i2c3 then never probes, dw-hdmi's component bind defers forever waiting for
+ * the adapter named by ddc-i2c-bus, and display-subsystem never completes -
+ * it just re-binds the two VOPs in a loop, leaves /dev/dri absent and the
+ * HDMI output black. Nothing in the log points at the pin: the deferral is
+ * silent and the pinctrl line looks like an unrelated i2c complaint.
+ *
+ * This is the usual shape of this port's problems - a mainline-derived board
+ * dts on a vendor BSP dtsi. Mainline's rk3399.dtsi leaves DDC to the board;
+ * Rockchip's claims it in the SoC dtsi. Overriding pinctrl-0 here to CEC only
+ * lets i2c3 have the pins.
+ */
 &hdmi {
 	ddc-i2c-bus = <&i2c3>;
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&hdmi_cec>;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
@@ -247,6 +445,126 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * HDMI IN audio.
+ *
+ * The receiver hands the incoming HDMI audio out as I2S, and until now
+ * nothing on the SoC side listened: i2s0 was "disabled", so the board came
+ * up with "no soundcards found" while the TC358749 sat there producing a
+ * perfectly good 48 kHz stereo stream. tc35874x_set_hdmi_audio() already
+ * does the receiver half - clears FORCE_MUTE, selects I2S framing, sets
+ * AUDCHNUM_2 - so all that was ever missing was somewhere for the samples
+ * to go.
+ *
+ * The obvious wiring, receiver -> i2s0, is not this board's wiring. Page 25
+ * of the schematic has two separate buses that only look alike by name:
+ *
+ *   I2S0_*_HDMIIN   TC358749 -> ALC5651 port 2 (DACDAT2/LRCK2/BCLK2)
+ *   I2S0_*          RK3399   -> ALC5651 port 1 (via the 33R R7107..R7112)
+ *
+ * They never meet. The receiver's I2S goes into the codec, not into the
+ * SoC, and I2S_CLK_HDMIIN lands on test point TP50271 and stops there. So
+ * the path from HDMI to a capture buffer runs *through* the ALC5651:
+ *
+ *   TC358749 --I2S--> ALC5651 AIF2 (codec is clock slave; the receiver
+ *                     drives BCLK2/LRCK2)
+ *            internal digital loopback, no analogue detour:
+ *                     IF2 DAC -> DAC L2/R2 Mux -> DD MIX
+ *                             -> Stereo1 ADC MIX -> IF1 ADC1
+ *   ALC5651 --I2S--> RK3399 i2s0 SDI0 (SoC is clock master; it drives
+ *                    BCLK1/LRCK1 and the codec's MCLK off GPIO4_A0)
+ *
+ * That is why this node makes the *cpu* side both bitclock- and
+ * frame-master, and why i2s0 takes the full i2s0_2ch_bus: SCLK, LRCK_RX,
+ * LRCK_TX, SDI0, SDO0 and - the one that matters most - GPIO4_A0/I2S_CLK,
+ * the codec's MCLK. Nothing else on the board drives that pin, so leaving
+ * it out leaves the codec without a system clock.
+ *
+ * The two clock domains either side of the loopback are genuinely
+ * independent (the receiver's 48 kHz is not the SoC's), so the codec's ASRC
+ * has to bridge them. That is a runtime setting, not a device tree one -
+ * see the mixer setup shipped with the image.
+ *
+ * The "HDMIIN" microphone widget exists only to give the codec's
+ * "AIF2 Playback" DAI widget a DAPM source. Without it that widget has no
+ * incoming path (nothing binds rt5651-aif2 to a dai_link), the walk from
+ * "AIF1 Capture" back through IF2 DAC finds no endpoint, and DAPM leaves
+ * the whole loopback powered down - the capture would run and return
+ * silence. It is the same trick the vendor 4.4 machine driver
+ * (rockchip_rt5651_tc358749x.c) used, for the same reason.
+ *
+ * The card is called "tc358743" even though the part fitted here is a
+ * TC358749 and the card is really the ALC5651. The name is an interface,
+ * not a description: "hw:tc358743,0" is what kvmd's stock
+ * /etc/kvmd/janus/janus.plugin.ustreamer.jcfg opens, that file is a pacman
+ * backup file, and an upstream change to it arrives as a .pacnew whose
+ * usual three-way merge would silently drop any edit of ours and take
+ * WebRTC audio with it. Matching the name kvmd already expects means there
+ * is nothing to edit and nothing to lose. docs/hardware.md says which part
+ * is actually fitted.
+ */
+/ {
+	hdmiin_sound: hdmiin-sound {
+		compatible = "simple-audio-card";
+		status = "okay";
+
+		simple-audio-card,name = "tc358743";
+		simple-audio-card,format = "i2s";
+		simple-audio-card,mclk-fs = <256>;
+		simple-audio-card,bitclock-master = <&hdmiin_cpu_dai>;
+		simple-audio-card,frame-master = <&hdmiin_cpu_dai>;
+
+		simple-audio-card,widgets =
+			"Microphone", "HDMIIN",
+			"Headphone", "Headphone Jack",
+			"Line", "Line Out";
+		simple-audio-card,routing =
+			"AIF2 Playback", "HDMIIN",
+			"Headphone Jack", "HPOL",
+			"Headphone Jack", "HPOR",
+			"Line Out", "LOUTL",
+			"Line Out", "LOUTR";
+
+		hdmiin_cpu_dai: simple-audio-card,cpu {
+			sound-dai = <&i2s0>;
+		};
+
+		simple-audio-card,codec {
+			sound-dai = <&rt5651>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * i2s0_2ch_bus is the stock rk3399.dtsi group and already covers every pin
+ * this link needs, GPIO4_A0/I2S_CLK included; i2s0_8ch_bus, which the node
+ * defaults to, does not - it spends the extra pins on SDI1..3/SDO1..3 and
+ * leaves MCLK unmuxed.
+ */
+&i2s0 {
+	pinctrl-names = "bclk_on", "bclk_off";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2s0_2ch_bus>;
+	pinctrl-1 = <&i2s0_2ch_bus_bclk_off>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+/*
+ * The ALC5651 is register-compatible with the RT5651 - the device ID at
+ * 0xff reads 0x6281, which is what rt5651_i2c_probe() insists on - so the
+ * mainline "realtek,rt5651" binding drives it as-is. mclk is the SoC's
+ * SCLK_I2S_8CH_OUT, whose source mux (CLKSEL_CON31[1:0]) picks clk_i2s0 at
+ * reset, so it follows the rate i2s0 is running at with nothing to assign.
+ */
+&i2c1 {
+	rt5651: audio-codec@1a {
+		compatible = "realtek,rt5651";
+		reg = <0x1a>;
+		clocks = <&cru SCLK_I2S_8CH_OUT>;
+		clock-names = "mclk";
+		#sound-dai-cells = <0>;
+	};
+};
+
 &i2c0 {
 	clock-frequency = <400000>;
 	i2c-scl-rising-time-ns = <168>;
@@ -276,7 +594,23 @@
 		vcc10-supply = <&vcc3v3_sys>;
 		vcc11-supply = <&vcc3v3_sys>;
 		vcc12-supply = <&vcc3v3_sys>;
-		vddio-supply = <&vcc_3v0>;
+		/*
+		 * vddio-supply deliberately dropped.
+		 *
+		 * It pointed at &vcc_3v0, which is this PMIC's own LDO_REG8 - the
+		 * chip declared as its own I/O supply. The 4.4 tree says the same
+		 * thing and does not care, because fw_devlink did not exist yet.
+		 * 6.12 builds device links from these properties, spots the loop,
+		 * and reports:
+		 *
+		 *   /i2c@ff3c0000/pmic@1b: Fixed dependency cycle(s) with
+		 *     /i2c@ff3c0000/pmic@1b/regulators/LDO_REG8
+		 *
+		 * "Fixed" means it cut a link to break the loop, and consumers
+		 * downstream of the cut wait forever: dw_mci_init_slot() keeps
+		 * returning -EPROBE_DEFER on vqmmc-supply and the SD card never
+		 * enumerates.
+		 */
 
 		regulators {
 			vdd_center: DCDC_REG1 {
@@ -364,6 +698,12 @@
 				regulator-boot-on;
 				regulator-min-microvolt = <1800000>;
 				regulator-max-microvolt = <3400000>;
+				/*
+				 * Also for the BSP U-Boot: this is the SD
+				 * card's I/O rail, and the 4.4 tree brings it
+				 * up at 3.0 V before touching the card.
+				 */
+				regulator-init-microvolt = <3000000>;
 				regulator-state-mem {
 					regulator-on-in-suspend;
 					regulator-suspend-microvolt = <3000000>;
@@ -542,7 +882,9 @@
 
 		typec_con: connector {
 			compatible = "usb-c-connector";
-			data-role = "host";
+			/* device, not host: this port emulates HID and mass
+			 * storage to the target. See &usbdrd_dwc3_0 below. */
+			data-role = "device";
 			label = "USB-C";
 			op-sink-microwatt = <1000000>;
 			power-role = "dual";
@@ -593,6 +935,30 @@
 };
 
 &pinctrl {
+	/*
+	 * HDMI IN rails and control lines; without these the bridge is dark.
+	 *
+	 * RK_PA7 is the bridge's reset (driven by its reset-gpios) and RK_PB4
+	 * is its interrupt, so both are left as plain inputs for their
+	 * consumers to take over - the rails are the only thing this group
+	 * has to hold. RK_PB4 used to be pcfg_output_high here, which
+	 * contradicted the interrupts property on the same node; it worked
+	 * only because rockchip_irq_set_type() clears the line's port_ddr bit
+	 * and turns it back into an input behind pinctrl's back.
+	 */
+	hdmiin {
+		hdmiin_gpios: hdmiin-gpios {
+			rockchip,pins =
+				<2 RK_PA5 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_output_high>,
+				<2 RK_PA6 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_output_high>,
+				<2 RK_PA7 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_pull_none>,
+				<2 RK_PB0 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_output_high>,
+				<2 RK_PB1 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_output_high>,
+				<2 RK_PB2 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_output_low>,
+				<2 RK_PB4 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_pull_none>;
+		};
+	};
+
 	buttons {
 		pwr_btn: pwr-btn {
 			rockchip,pins = <0 RK_PA5 RK_FUNC_GPIO &pcfg_pull_up>;
@@ -740,6 +1106,27 @@
 		interrupt-names = "host-wake";
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 		pinctrl-0 = <&wifi_host_wake_l>;
+
